.TH SX 1 "3 October 2025" 4.0
.SH NAME
sx \- start an x server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sx
.RI [ "command " [ arguments ]...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sx
can be used to replace both
.BR xinit (1)
and
.BR startx (1)
for starting a
.BR Xorg (1)
with an initial client.  By default
.B sx
will attempt to execute
.I \%XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sx/sxrc
unless a
.I command
is provided.  The
.I command
may have additional
.IR arguments .
.PP
.B sx
can additionally execute
.I XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sx/sxsv
to start an X server with custom command line arguments if it is executable.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B sx
will attempt to inherit the exit status from the X server. If
.B sx
was signalled then the exit status will reflect this, otherwise it will be
zero.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DISPLAY
The display name used for the X server that is provided for
.IR sxrc .
.TP
.B XAUTHORITY
This environment references a file which stores authorisation entries
used to secure the X server. If this environment is not set then
.B sx
will create and use
.I \%XDG_DATA_HOME/sx/xauthority
instead while also exporting this value to
.BR XAUTHORITY .
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
The directory used when searching for
.I sxrc
and
.IR sxsv .
.B \%HOME/.config
will be used if not set.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
The directory used for storing the
.I xauthority
file.
.B \%HOME/.local/share
will be used if not set.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sx/sxrc
The default command started by
.BR sx .
This file must be executable.
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sx/sxsv
The file used instead of
.BR Xorg (1).
The first argument will be the display number and the
.B XAUTHORITY
environment will made be available. This file must be executable.
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME/sx/xauthority
The default authority used by
.B sx
if an alternative file is not provided with
.BR XAUTHORITY .
.SH NOTES
With the addition of
.BR Xorg.wrap (1)
it may be necessary to set
.I allowed_users
to
.I anybody
in
.I \%/etc/X11/Xwrapper.config
if
.B sx
is used in a pipeline or its standard output and standard error is redirected
to a file as it will not be considered a console user.
.SH EXAMPLES
Use an existing
.I .Xinitrc
by specifying an appropriate interpreter such as
.BR sh (1)
or making it executable with a correct interpreter line:
.IP
.EX
.B sx sh ~/.Xinitrc
.EE
.PP
Pass arguments to the standard
.I sxrc
by presenting it to
.B sx
as a
.IR command :
.IP
.EX
.B sx ~/.config/sx/sxrc arg1 arg2
.EE
.PP
Specify custom XKEYBOARD extension options using
.IR \%XDG_CONFIG_HOME/sx/sxsv :
.IP
.EX
.B #!/bin/sh \-\-
.B Xorg :\[dq]$1\[dq] vt\[dq]$1\[dq] -keeptty -noreset -auth \[dq]$XAUTHORITY\[dq] -ardelay 200 -arinterval 20
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.UR https://github.com/Earnestly/sx
.UE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR startx (1),
.BR xinit (1),
.BR Xorg (1),
.BR X (7),
.BR Xsecurity (7),
.BR Xwrapper.config (5)
